The proof of Theorem \ref{T: single box norm bound} begins with PET induction, a complexity-reduction argument that allows us to control the counting operator of a polynomial progression by an average of box norms.  While PET arguments are now standard and have been carried out in many places (see, e.g., \cite{Ber87, BL96, DFKS22, Pel20, Pre17}), we need to keep careful track of the structure of the polynomial differencing parameters ${\textbf{C}}_j$ that are output by PET.  In general, the input of PET is the counting operator lower bound
\begin{align*}
    {\sum_{{\textbf{x}}\in{\mathbb Z}^D}\mathbb{E}_{z\in[N^{1/d}]} f_0({\textbf{x}})\cdot f_1({\textbf{x}}+{\textbf{P}}_1(z))\cdots f_\ell({\textbf{x}}+{\textbf{P}}_\ell(z))} \geq \delta N^D,
\end{align*}
for some polynomials ${\textbf{P}}_1, \ldots, {\textbf{P}}_\ell\in{\mathbb Z}^D[z]$ of degree at most $d$ and some $1$-bounded functions $f_0, \ldots, f_\ell$ supported on $[N]^D$.  The output of PET is then that the average of box norms 
\begin{align}\label{E: average of box norms of polynomials}
	 \mathbb{E}_{{\underline{h}}\in[\pm N^{1/d}]^{r}}\norm{f}_{{{\textbf{C}}_1({\underline{h}})\cdot[\pm N^{1/d}]}, \ldots, {{\textbf{C}}_s({\underline{h}})\cdot[\pm N^{1/d}]}}^{2^s} \gg \delta^{O(1)} N^D
\end{align}
is also large. (Here and elsewhere, we allow all $O(1)$ terms to depend on $d,\ell$.)  Two crucial properties of this output are that the polynomials ${\textbf{C}}_1, \ldots, {\textbf{C}}_s\in{\mathbb Z}^D[h_1, \ldots, h_r]$ appearing above are multilinear and that their coefficients are $O(1)$-multiples of the leading coefficients of ${\textbf{P}}_1, \ldots, {\textbf{P}}_\ell$ and of their pairwise differences.  This PET argument is carried out in detail in Section \ref{S: PET}.

\begin{align*}
    {\sum_{{\textbf{x}}\in{\mathbb Z}^D}\mathbb{E}_{z\in[N^{1/d}]} f_0({\textbf{x}})\cdot f_1({\textbf{x}}+{\textbf{P}}_1(z))\cdots f_\ell({\textbf{x}}+{\textbf{P}}_\ell(z))} \geq \delta N^D,
\end{align*}

\begin{align}\label{E: average of box norms of polynomials}
	 \mathbb{E}_{{\underline{h}}\in[\pm N^{1/d}]^{r}}\norm{f}_{{{\textbf{C}}_1({\underline{h}})\cdot[\pm N^{1/d}]}, \ldots, {{\textbf{C}}_s({\underline{h}})\cdot[\pm N^{1/d}]}}^{2^s} \gg \delta^{O(1)} N^D
\end{align}

\begin{align}\label{E: PET output}
    \mathbb{E}_{{\underline{h}}\in[\pm H]^{r}}\norm{f}_{{{\textbf{C}}_1({\underline{h}})\cdot[\pm M]}, \ldots, {{\textbf{C}}_s({\underline{h}})\cdot[\pm M]}}^{2^s} \gg \delta N^D,
